+#### `hold`
+
+Creates a hold for a scope (see [Holds and pinned coins](#holds-and-pinned-coins)). Parameters:
+
+- the requesting scope, derived by the wallet
+- a selection: either explicit references to own final coins, or a map of token type to amount, in which case the
+  wallet selects final, unpinned coins covering each amount using its ordinary coin selection
+- a policy: `expires_at`, optional `tag` and `note`
+
+Steps:
+
+1. Resolve the selection to a set of own **final, unpinned** coins; fail if it cannot be satisfied (the error MUST NOT
+   reveal whether coins pinned by other holds would have satisfied it)
+2. Validate the policy against wallet limits (maximum duration, maximum number of holds per scope, metadata size)
+3. Atomically create the hold, mark the coins `pinned`, and persist
+4. Return the hold identifier
+
+Holds MUST be persisted with the rest of the wallet state and MUST be restored before any spend is allowed after a
+restart. If the persisted hold state cannot be read or validated, the wallet MUST refuse ordinary spends rather than
+treat the coins as unpinned.
+
+A wallet MAY require user confirmation for creating a hold, exactly as it may for any spend; a hold is a commitment of
+funds and SHOULD be presented as such (amount, token type, requesting scope, expiry).
+
+#### `detach_transaction`
+
+Hands a transaction built with an authorization off to the hold's scope as a candidate. It is the counterpart of
+`discard_transaction` for the case where the wallet is *not* the submitter. Steps:
+
+1. Verify the transaction is in the pending pool and was built under an authorization for the given hold
+2. Record the candidate: identifying components, coins it would create, validity bound, status `detached`
+3. Remove the transaction from the pending pool; un-book its inputs so that they return to `pinned`; move the coins
+   it would create from the pending set to the candidate's expected set
+4. Any unpinned coin the build had to add is already pinned in the hold (see `spend`); verify this invariant
+5. Persist atomically, then return the serialized transaction to the scope
+
+After `detach_transaction` the same coins can be used again by another authorized build for the same hold, which is
+how several mutually exclusive candidates are produced from one set of coins. The wallet SHOULD warn the user, when
+presenting a hold, that candidates are outside the wallet's control: they may be submitted by the scope at any time
+before they become stale, and local release does not revoke them.
+
+#### `release_hold`
+
+Ends a hold. Steps:
+
+1. Verify the scope owns the hold (or the request comes from the wallet user)
+2. Mark every candidate `released`
+3. Return every pinned coin of the hold with no live booking to `final`
+4. Persist atomically
+
+The wallet MUST make it clear that released candidates may still be valid on chain until their validity bound passes
+or their inputs are spent. A scope or user that needs a guarantee that no candidate can settle any more MUST spend the
+coins (for example, to the wallet itself) after the release; the wallet MAY offer this as a "cancel" convenience that
+performs the release and the self-transfer together.
+
+A hold whose `expires_at` has passed is released automatically. Expiry is evaluated against the wallet clock and
+SHOULD include the same latency margin used for TTL handling.
+
